INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - SUPPLY CLOSET TURNED INTERVIEW ROOM - DAY

A narrow room, mop buckets pushed to one side. A card table. Three folding chairs. A single window painted shut.

PUBLIC DEFENDER NOREEN ASANTE-WHITFIELD (40s) sits with a file open. Across from her, the prosecutor ELLIS PORTILLO-ADEBAYO (40s) pushes a paper across the table. Between them, in the third chair, their juror-turned-witness DESMOND KORKMAZ-LIU (30s) grips the seat edge.

Noreen slides the paper back.

NOREEN
No.

ELLIS
You haven't read it.

NOREEN
I've read it.

ELLIS
It's a good offer.

NOREEN
It's a bad one.

Ellis taps the paper. She pushes back. He lets her.

ELLIS
Mr. Korkmaz-Liu, you understand why we're here?

DESMOND
I was juror number six.

NOREEN
You were.

ELLIS
You spoke to the defendant's sister during recess.

DESMOND
She spoke to me.

NOREEN
(to Desmond)
What did she say?

DESMOND
She said her brother didn't do it.

ELLIS
That's inadmissible.

^NOREEN
That's inadmissible.

They glare at each other. Desmond looks from one to the other.

ELLIS
I didn't mean...

NOREEN
Neither did I.

They both lean back. The folding chairs creak. Noreen closes the file. Ellis straightens the offer. Desmond lets go of his seat. The three of them sit in silence, waiting for someone to move first.

ELLIS
Mr. Korkmaz-Liu, thank you for your honesty. You may go.

DESMOND
That's it?

NOREEN
That's it.

Desmond stands, bumps a mop bucket, and flees. Ellis and Noreen watch the door swing shut.

ELLIS
Offer stands till five.

NOREEN
I'll see you in court.
